\begin{frame}{Basic Introduction}
Nonlinear dynamical systems appear in many physical systems such as:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Planetary motion
  \item Fluid dynamics
  \item Robotics
  \item Control systems
\end{itemize}
\vspace{0.3cm}
Traditional analysis is difficult because nonlinear systems cannot usually be solved analytically.

\vspace{0.3cm}
Koopman operator theory provides a way to analyze nonlinear systems using linear techniques.
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Linear System and Its Conditions}
\textbf{Linear system:} A system where the output changes in a perfectly proportional and predictable way with respect to the input.

\vspace{0.4cm}
A linear system behaves in a straightforward, proportional, and additive manner.

\vspace{0.4cm}
A linear system will always follow two laws:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Law of Homogeneity
  \item Law of Additivity
\end{enumerate}

\vspace{0.3cm}
A Nonlinear system will not follow either of the above conditions.\\
Most real-world systems are Nonlinear due to their unpredictable nature.
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Laws of Linear Systems}
\textbf{1) Law of Homogeneity:}\\
If an input is multiplied by a constant, the output must also be multiplied by the same constant.
\[
  T(ax) = a\,T(x) \quad \forall\; \text{scalar } a
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}
\textbf{2) Law of Additivity:}\\
If two inputs are applied separately and produce two outputs, then applying both inputs together should produce the sum of those outputs.
\[
  T(x_1 + x_2) = T(x_1) + T(x_2)
\]
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Problem With Nonlinear Systems}
\textbf{Linear systems:}
\[
  \dot{x} = Ax
\]
\textbf{Closed-form solution:}
\[
  x(t) = e^{At}x_0
\]

\vspace{0.4cm}
Nonlinear systems generally do not have closed-form solutions.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Why?} The $\sin(\theta)$ term in the pendulum, or $x_1 x_2$ cross-terms, cannot be solved by matrix exponentials alone.

\vspace{0.3cm}
Therefore new analysis tools are required.
\end{frame}

% ============================================================
%  SECTION 2 — KOOPMAN OPERATOR CONCEPT
% ============================================================

\begin{frame}{Koopman Operator Concept}
Instead of studying the state $x$ directly, Koopman theory studies \textbf{functions of the state}.

\vspace{0.3cm}
Define an \textbf{observable} (measurement function):
\[
  g : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}, \quad g = g(x)
\]

\vspace{0.3cm}
Example observables:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $g(x) = x_1$ \hfill (identity — the state itself)
  \item $g(x) = x_1^2$ \hfill (quadratic)
  \item $g(x) = \sin(x_1)$ \hfill (trigonometric)
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Key idea:} Even though $x$ evolves nonlinearly, $g(x)$ may evolve \emph{linearly} if we choose $g$ correctly.
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Koopman Operator — Definition}
For a nonlinear discrete-time system $x_{k+1} = F(x_k)$, the \textbf{Koopman operator} $\mathcal{K}$ is defined as:
\[
  \boxed{(\mathcal{K}g)(x) = g(F(x)) = g(x_{k+1})}
\]

\vspace{0.3cm}
$\mathcal{K}$ maps any observable $g$ to its value one time-step in the future.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textbf{Key property:} Even if $F$ is nonlinear,
\[
  \mathcal{K}(ag_1 + bg_2) = a\mathcal{K}g_1 + b\mathcal{K}g_2
\]
The Koopman operator is \textbf{always linear} — at the cost of acting on an infinite-dimensional function space.
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Infinite-Dimensional Nature}
The Koopman operator acts on the full space of observables $\mathcal{G}$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
This space is \textbf{infinite-dimensional} — for any nonlinear system, closure forces you to keep adding new functions to the dictionary (see later slides).

\vspace{0.5cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
 & \textbf{State-space view} & \textbf{Koopman view} \\
\midrule
Space & $\mathbb{R}^n$ (finite) & $\mathcal{G}$ (infinite) \\
Dynamics & Nonlinear $F$ & Linear $\mathcal{K}$ \\
Analysis tools & Limited & Full linear algebra \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.4cm}
In practice: choose a \textbf{finite dictionary} $\Psi(x) \in \mathbb{R}^N$ and approximate $\mathcal{K}$ on that subspace using data.
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Need for Koopman Operator}
\textbf{We saw:} Real-world systems are nonlinear. Nonlinear systems are difficult to analyze.

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textbf{Linear systems are easy} because we can use:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Eigenvalues and spectral theory
  \item Stability tools (Routh–Hurwitz, Lyapunov)
  \item Matrix methods (LQR, Kalman filter)
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{The Problem:} We need linear tools for easy computation but the system is nonlinear.

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textbf{The Idea:} Instead of making the system linear in \emph{state space}, we make it linear in \emph{function space}.
\[
  \underbrace{\dot{x} = f(x)}_{\text{nonlinear in } \mathbb{R}^n}
  \;\longrightarrow\;
  \underbrace{\dot{\Psi} = \mathcal{K}\Psi}_{\text{linear in } \mathcal{G}}
\]
\end{frame}

